; 22-break.kurt

; `break` discards the current block immediately -- no proof step, nothing
; added anywhere -- without needing to dedent past it first the way
; 15-sandbox.kurt's plain dedent did.  It's for when you already know you
; want to abandon a block right now, rather than writing something at a
; shallower indentation to trigger the close.

prop A
use A

sandbox
    use false
    break            ; abandon the sandbox here, instead of dedenting out of it

A                   ; still fine, `A` is a real axiom from outside the sandbox
; false             ; would fail: `false` only ever existed inside the sandbox

; `break` works on any open block, not just `sandbox` -- it always gives up
; the innermost open thing. In the `proof` of a `show`, that is the `proof`:
; the claim stays open, and a new `proof` can try again (no `show` to repeat):

load propositional
show A implies A
proof
    break            ; this attempt is given up -- the claim `A implies A` is still open
proof
    assume A
        A
qed

; Right after a `show` (or after the `break` of its `proof`), no block is open
; for the claim -- so the innermost open thing is the claim itself, and `break`
; withdraws it. `break`, `break` gives up a proof completely -- the claim is
; still to do (the file ends with "Proof almost checked", the claim as a `todo`):

show A and not A
proof
    break            ; the proof
break                ; and the claim -- nothing is left open, nothing was added

; A `show` that is neither proved nor withdrawn is an error at the end of the
; file ("the claim ... has no proof").
